  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 107 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nSurface and satellite observations show clearing cloud cover over\nthe region, removing a days-long area of stratus. Sunshine will\nbegin to filter through the clouds, leading to a slightly warmer\nday today. However, northerly flow behind last week's front is\nstill present which will keep highs in the upper 70s to low 80s.\nA few showers will stick around across northeastern Mississippi,\nbut will not produce much in terms of accumulating precipitation.\nA cold front associated with a trough currently over Ontario will\nswing south through tonight leading to a punch of dry air and\nfurther cooling. This will lead to very pleasant weather Monday\nand Tuesday with light winds, highs in the 70s and low 80s, and\nlow humidity.\n\nThe upper pattern will return to a more benign, zonal state over\nthe southern CONUS into the middle of the week, keeping the\nregion dry. At the surface, cold advection will begin to weaken\nby Tuesday, leading to a gradual increase in temperatures through\nthe end of the week. Expect high temperatures to climb back into\nthe 80s, reaching into the upper 80s by Thursday. On the other\nhand, dry air will remain throughout the region, leading to\nafternoon relative humidity values around 30% or lower starting\nWednesday, likely lasting through the end of the week. So,\nelevated fire weather conditions will exist through the end of\nnext week.\n\nWhile the above is occurring, the original front from last week\nwill settle across the Gulf, transitioning into a stationary\nboundary. In the past 24 hours, models have started to introduce\nthe idea of a tropical system forming along the stationary front\noff the eastern coast of Mexico, eventually dragging it northward\ntowards the CONUS along the Gulf coastline in MS/AL/FL. While\nmodels have indicated the potential for a landfalling tropical\ncyclone, there is not yet a clear consensus amongst either\nensemble or deterministic guidance on if there will be a tropical\ncyclone or its strength. However, the presence of tropical\nactivity would have large implications on the last few days of\nthe forecast period, but with current uncertainties it is still\ntoo early to pin down exact impacts for the Mid-South.",
